    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 all 36 tests pass, including the new `Cp26Tests` (national-length validation) and the existing `Cp08Tests` (E.164 normalization).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number-length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerTelephone.java` (the shared telephone normalizer used by the owner create/update pipelines).\n\n**The change** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerTelephone.java`):\n\n- Added a lookup of known country codes to their required national-number length: `+61` \u2192 9 digits, `+1` \u2192 10 digits.\n- After forming the E.164 string and applying the general 8\u201315 total-digit bound, it now checks the matching country code: if the national number (digits after the country code) doesn't have the exact expected length, it throws `InvalidOwnerTelephoneException`, which is handled globally as a **400**.\n\n**Why it's correct and safe:**\n- `\"+61 123\"` \u2192 `+61123` has 3 national digits \u2260 9, so it's rejected with 400 (the new `Cp26Tests` case).\n- Unknown country codes (e.g. the existing `Cp08` `+64...` case \u2192 8 national digits) are left unconstrained, so no existing behavior regresses.\n- The `+61`/`+1` prefixes don't overlap (no number starts with both), so the match-and-break loop is unambiguous.\n- Valid owners from `AcceptanceBase.uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) still pass, keeping every other checkpoint's payload valid.",
